		<div class="post" id="post-3886722">
		<div class="long">
			<div class="head">
									<div class="options">
						<a href="javascript:;" onclick="togglePostFold(event,3886722)" class="btn btn-default btn-small btn-sm">Fold</a>
					</div>
								<div class="title" id="post-title-3886722">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/jacob-conwell" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1372582); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1372582&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607200940" alt="Jacob Conwell" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1372582)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/jacob-conwell" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1372582); return false;" >Jacob Conwell</a></span> <span class="odate time_1532632284 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">26 Jul 2018 19:11</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3886722">
									

<p>Effective worldbuilding in an article can be a beautiful thing. It can make the world of the SCP Foundation come to life and seem like a real place, with rules, history, and its own clashes and ideals. This is what makes pieces like WrongJohnSilver's 001 or the TwistedGears/djkaktus 001 so effective. Poor worldbuilding, on the other hand, typically ends up being exposition dumps. While they often do establish their own world, they bog themselves down and lose the reader in the resulting quagmire. I'm afraid this article was the latter for me.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>The object itself is fairly interesting. A massive combat sub built into a giant hermit crab is quite a spectacle to behold, and I do like the first diagram explaining the various layers of the interior. This gives the article some depth and makes the object seem like an explored place. The second picture breaks my immersion a little, since I've seen that illustration around the net before, and I hesitate to believe it would be included in an SCP document.</p>
<p>Then we get down to the supporting documents. The News Articles after a while just blend into a long exposition dump. You have an entire civilization discovered and the only interesting/noteworthy documents the SCP Foundation can find are newspapers and maybe one or two other document styles? On top of that, the history you tell here is a little lukewarm to me as a reader. Perhaps I would find more fondness here if I was a little more invested in your Finnfolk canon, but as is going through those is a bit like watching a Star Wars movie and pausing every now and then to read an ongoing history about the Hutts. While I am sure that will draw some fans, it's not the reason I am here to see the film.</p>
<p>The exploration logs prove problematic in their own way. With dialogue gems like:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>R-5: &quot;I got a bad feeling about all of this.&quot;</p>
<p>R-3: &quot;You get bad feelings about everything.&quot;</p>
<p>R-5: &quot;They usually aren't wrong!&quot;</p>
<p>R-3: &quot;That's because you're a walking cliche 90% of the time.&quot;</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>R-2: &quot;At the end of pointy sticks again.&quot;</p>
<p>R-Cap: &quot;Reminds me of that mission in Micronesia.&quot;</p>
<p>R-5: &quot;Aren't we not supposed to talk about that?&quot;</p>
<p>R-Cap: &quot;You aren't.&quot;</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>R-6: &quot;Who are you?&quot;</p>
<p>Matriarch: &quot;I'm Ragnhild the Mad.&quot; [Five seconds of cackling.]</p>
<p>R-5: &quot;I'll say.&quot;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>These read more like the kind of things people might say to their friends while gathered around to play Dungeons and Dragons than believable dialogue from hardened MTF agents, or the elders of ancient and powerful civilizations. It just kills the immersion. On top of that, each of these logs reads like a tale. Had you made them their own tale series I think it might have worked out a little more in your favor, as then you could really get into imagery to describe your world and bring it to life. As is, however, the clinical tone and format of a log suck out that opportunity and we are just left with the dialogue and some dry description of actions.</p>
<p>Finally, you fall prey to Deus Ex Machina at the end. You build up the monsters of SCP-3700 quite a bit in that article and really do a good job justifying the Hiemal object class. Having one of them attack at the end here is a bold move, but instead of making use of the massive battle sub you have spent nearly 12K words building up, you have an outside force swoop in to save the day.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>I really want to like this. I can tell a lot of blood, sweat, and tears was poured into it to bring it to life. Unfortunatley, the problems listed above just cause the whole thing to fail to solidfy, and the length does the rest to kill the experience. At +44 at the time of writing, its clear you have an audience here on the site for the article, but I don't think I'm part of it.</p>
<p>-1.</p>

							</div>

								
					
