			<div class="content" id="post-content-3891466">
									

<p>This criticism is fair and appreciated. I'm going to do my best to adjust and fix the pieces that you've pointed out made this less enjoyable. I've already made a massive change to the ending to remove the perceived Deus Ex Machina. As is, the recovered materials are not something I can fix as immediately, as I will have to carefully consider how to diversify and improve the individual pieces of recovered material.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Show&nbsp;Response&nbsp;to&nbsp;Criticism</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Hide&nbsp;Response</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>In order to shed light on portions of this, I feel it necessary to explain some of the motivation behind pieces that were included. The recovered materials were constructed in order to give the reader space to breath between the logs. You are the first, though I'm sure not the only, person to leave feedback in a way that indicates this was problematic, and/or you didn't enjoy the story being woven by the logs, which is fair.</p>
<p>They are, somewhat heavily, leaning towards the side of exposition, which is a failure on my part. At the time that they were written, they were meant to serve the breathing room purpose and the primary vector for the history. I could perhaps have improved upon that structure, with more personalized documents and recovered materials, personal items, etc. Collected by the Foundation as they are exploring, to more indirectly/less expositionally demonstrate the events leading to the state the Finnfolk are currently in. This comes down to a lack of time, and a lack of necessary direction to see the exposition-heavy and lukewarm nature of the materials.</p>
<p>I understand the dialogue complaint, cause its a fairly common one on the site. I don't entirely agree with the criticism, especially in the face of a team of experienced agents who've, as to put it, &quot;been through the shitter.&quot; The characters are written the way they are because they're human. Because they've been through <em>hell</em> and how they cope with it, as a group that has chemistry, is by bantering. I went so far as to directly address this, in a not so subtle manner.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>R-2: &quot;Of course we do, just look out at the city. It's all a big angry problem.&quot;</p>
<p>R-Cap: &quot;Roger command, we're reading.&quot; [To R-2] &quot;Jane, cut the sarcasm to command. Save the banter for the field.&quot;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The first example you provided was a suggestion made to inject more humor into the article, the second example has been praised by a number of different authors and users, and the last leads into a vicious response to a lack of respect on the part of R-5. I will probably do more editing to the most offending pieces of dialogue, but I'm relatively unwilling to budge on the bantering/somewhat loose and unprofessional nature of the Recon team members, or the eccentric behavior of Ragnhild, as those elements have received very positive responses from a spread of different users, and I'm afraid if I remove them, the logs will fall victim to the same issue the incident log from 3456 fell into.</p>
<p>With that said,</p>
<blockquote>
<p>On top of that, each of these logs reads like a tale. Had you made them their own tale series I think it might have worked out a little more in your favor, as then you could really get into imagery to describe your world and bring it to life.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is the shortcoming of the medium, and obviously a pitfall of logs in general, but also a result of attempting to restrain my desire to describe every succinct detail (Knowing what is and isn't important, and being able to discern and prioritize elements over others is something I struggle with not just in writing, but everywhere. I've gotten into the habit of describing less rather than more). Several times, while I was giving feedback, Roget suggested small tweaks to give the reader a better visualization of what was happening/improve upon the imagery. I would <em>love</em> to know if there are specific spots or locations in the logs where I could do that in order to provide the reader with a better image of what is happening, and thus, better engage them. Clinical tone isn't the best medium for conveying imagery, but setting the stage in the right manner, its possible to paint a beautiful mental image, and any guidance therein where those images can be improved is appreciated.</p>
<p>Finally, in regards to the image, describing the nature of the nested cylinders proved extremely difficult to do without visual aides. I hate that it broke immersion for you, I was unsure how else I could help the reader visualize the space, and chose to include an image of what it would, theoretically, most closely resemble. If enough people found it immersion breaking I can certainly remove it.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>I greatly appreciate the in-depth feedback, there are changes here that I can make and things I can improve on. I hate that you didn't enjoy it, and feel as if a number of these things I can fix and improve upon following the end of the 4000 contest. I've made significant efforts to fix the article's ending, and remove the Deus Ex-Machina, and replace it with a climactic battle, calling back to 3700, while demonstrating the capabilities of the machine that the entire article has built towards. It fixes one thing&#8230; and hopefully, I can fix the rest.</p>
<p>This response got long, man, I just write too much.</p>

							</div>

								
					
